---
layout: hub-app.njk
title: Wikidata queries as set browsing
appBase: /web/hub/wikidata-parallax
appNoScript: true
appCommonsImages: true
appSource: https://github.com/danbri/factoidal-core/tree/main/docs/web/hub/wikidata-parallax
appEngineLabel: Query explanations
appEngineUrl: ../wikidata-explanations/
permalink: /web/hub/wikidata-parallax/index.html
---
{%- import "wikidata-parallax-macros.njk" as m -%}
<div class="px">
  <header class="px-hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">Martin Poulter's Wikidata queries</p>
    <h1>Queries as set browsing</h1>
    <p class="lede">Many Wikidata queries can be done by browsing. Start from a set of things, narrow it with facets,
    then step along a property to a related set. That is how Freebase Parallax worked (Huynh, 2008).
    This page shows which of the {{ counts.classified }} parsed queries can be built that way, and draws each one as blocks.</p>

    <figure class="legend blocks" aria-label="How to read the blocks">
      <ol class="pz-row" aria-hidden="true">
        <li class="pz pz-set pz-hat"><span class="pz-title">every painting</span><span class="pz-in">collection <span class="eq">=</span> <b>Ashmolean Museum</b></span></li>
        <li class="pz pz-pivot">their <b>creator</b></li>
        <li class="pz pz-set"><span class="pz-title">creator</span><span class="pz-cols">shows date of birth</span></li>
      </ol>
      <figcaption>Each query is drawn as blocks that fit together, like Scratch. Start from a set (the block with the curved top),
      narrow it with facets (white), then pivot (purple) to a related set. For queries that need more than browsing,
      orange blocks hold chains together: <i>either … or …</i>, <i>leave out those where …</i>, <i>match these up</i>.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </header>

  <nav class="tiers" aria-label="Tiers">
    {%- for t in tiers %}
    <a class="tier-tile tile-{{ t.tier | lower }}" href="#tier-{{ t.tier | lower }}">
      <span class="tile-num">{{ t.ids | length }}</span>
      <span class="tile-name">{{ t.name }}</span>
    </a>
    {%- endfor %}
  </nav>
  <p class="note">{{ counts.withExclusions }} of the browsable queries use exclusions, and {{ counts.withExtensions }} use
  OR, path or grouping steps beyond classic Parallax. {{ counts.total - counts.classified }} queries could not be parsed and are not shown.</p>

  {%- for t in tiers %}
  <section class="tier-section" id="tier-{{ t.tier | lower }}" aria-labelledby="h-{{ t.tier | lower }}">
    <h2 id="h-{{ t.tier | lower }}">{{ m.tierBadge(t.tier) }} {{ t.name }} <span class="count">{{ t.ids | length }}</span></h2>
    <ul class="cards">
    {%- for q in queries %}{% if q.tier == t.tier %}
      {%- set lead = q.startId %}
      <li class="card">
        <a href="{{ q.id }}/">
          {{ m.thumb(lead, entities, thumb, 56, "thumb", q.start or q.title) | safe }}
          <span class="card-body">
            <span class="card-title">{{ q.title }}</span>
            <span class="card-meta">
              {%- if q.start %}from <b>{{ m.bare(q.start) }}</b>{% endif %}
              {%- if q.sets > 1 %} · {{ q.sets }} sets{% endif %}
              {%- for k in q.kinds %} · <span class="kind kind-{{ k }}">{{ {"exclude": "excludes", "or": "OR", "path": "paths", "derived": "grouped"}[k] }}</span>{% endfor %}
            </span>
            {%- if q.tier == "N" and q.flow.why | length %}<span class="card-why">{{ (q.flow.why | first).text }}</span>{% endif %}
          </span>
        </a>
      </li>
    {%- endif %}{% endfor %}
    </ul>
  </section>
  {%- endfor %}

  <footer class="px-about">
    <p>Each query is classified from its parsed Factoidal rewrite by rules (<code>explain/src/parallax.mjs</code>); no language model is used.
    Tier A is classic Parallax. B adds value filters or computed columns. C passes through statements and qualifiers, much like Freebase's
    mediator nodes. D uses subclasses to choose a type. An exclusion counts only if a single facet chip can show it.</p>
    <p>Queries: {{ corpus.attribution }}, <a href="{{ corpus.license_url }}">{{ corpus.license }}</a>. Labels from Wikidata ({{ sources.labels }}, CC0).
    Thumbnails come from Wikimedia Commons; follow an image to see its own licence. Generated {{ generated }}.</p>
  </footer>
</div>
